eval returns nothing relevant,\nthe correct behavior is to say so — before spending a model call — and flag the\nanswer for human review. Design the refusal path first; it is the path every other\nsafeguard falls back to.": "Agent Loops refer to the iterative process where an agent observes, decides, acts, and observes again, with the loop's reliability depending on how the process is engineered. Each iteration includes perceiving the context, deciding actions based on the model, acting through application execution, and observing results. Efficient loop design involves setting strict budgets, implementing stopping conditions, and prioritizing refusal as a key outcome to maintain integrity and prevent silent failures.",
  "Question: Summarize the document agent-loops.\n\nTool results so far:\nsummarize_document({\"doc_id\": \"agent-loops\"}) returned:\n[agent-loops] Agent Loops refer to the iterative process where an agent observes, decides, acts, and observes again, with the loop's reliability depending on how the process is engineered. Each iteration includes perceiving the context, deciding actions based on the model, acting through application execution, and observing results. Efficient loop design involves setting strict budgets, implementing stopping conditions, and prioritizing refusal as a key outcome to maintain integrity and prevent silent failures.": "{\"tool\": \"answer\", \"args\": {\"text\": \"Agent Loops are an iterative process involving observation, decision, action, and re-observation. Each iteration includes perceiving the context, deciding actions based on the model, acting through application execution, and observing results. Efficient loop design involves setting strict budgets, implementing stopping conditions, and prioritizing refusal to maintain integrity and prevent silent failures.\"}}",
